// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ngram data
//
// The built-in lists (bigrams, trigrams, tetragrams and common words)
// are ordered by frequency in written English, most frequent first.
// Only the first `top` entries of a list are used for a run, where
// `top` comes from the `-t/--top` command line argument (1..=200).
//
// Practising the most frequent combinations first is the whole point
// of ngram training: a relatively small set of letter sequences makes
// up a large share of everyday typing, so becoming fluent at those
// has an outsized effect on overall speed and confidence. The lists
// therefore start with combinations such as "th", "he" and "in" for
// bigrams, and the tail of each list contains progressively rarer
// combinations that are still worth drilling once the head of the
// list feels automatic.
//
// Instead of a built-in list, the user may point at a wordlist file.
// The file is read completely into memory and then processed line by
// line; every line is split at commas. Both of these files therefore
// describe the same three items:
//
//     alpha,bravo,charlie
//
//     alpha,bravo
//     charlie
//
// A few consequences of this intentionally simple format are worth
// remembering when authoring a wordlist:
//
//   * Commas separate items, lines merely group them. An item may
//     itself contain spaces, in which case the space is part of the
//     lesson text like any other character.
//   * Empty items are kept. A trailing comma, two adjacent commas,
//     or a blank line all produce empty strings in the list. During
//     lesson generation such an item contributes only its separator
//     space, which is harmless, if a little boring to type.
//   * Carriage returns at the end of a line (as in files written on
//     Windows) are stripped by the line iterator, so CRLF files work
//     as expected. A carriage return in the middle of a line is kept
//     and becomes part of the item.
//   * The file must be valid UTF-8. It is read with
//     `read_to_string`, so malformed input is rejected before the
//     terminal is even initialised.
//   * Only regular files are accepted. Passing a directory, a
//     device such as /dev/null, or a path that does not exist is
//     reported as "File not found" by the caller.
//
// During a lesson, `combi` items are picked at random (with
// replacement, so the same item can appear more than once), and the
// resulting block is repeated `rep` times. Because picks are made
// with replacement, asking for more combinations than the list
// holds (for example `-t 5 -c 200`) still works: items simply show
// up multiple times. The lesson text always terminates every item
// with a single space, including the very last one; typing that
// final space is what completes the lesson.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

"""
joined = doc.splitlines() + code
u=next(i for i,l in enumerate(joined) if "read_to_string(n).unwrap" in l)
print("unwrap at", u+1)
need=110-(u+1)
print("need pad",need)
if need>0:
